                    <img src="~/webassets/images/elements/step-28.webp" alt="" class="stack-back">
                </div>
            </div>
        </div><!-- Features Item -->

        <div class="row gy-4 align-items-stretch justify-content-between features-item" style="row-gap: 30px;">
            <div class="col-lg-6 d-flex align-items-center features-img-bg " data-aos="zoom-out">
                <img src="~/webassets/images/elements/step-36.webp" class="img-fluid" alt="">
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-5 d-flex justify-content-center flex-column " data-aos="fade-up">
                <ul>
                    <h4 class="pt-3 pb-1" style="font-size: 19px;">Whole Prey</h4>
                    <li>
                        Feeding your beloved feline companion whole prey is like treating them to a specially
                        curated feast that aligns perfectly with their natural instincts. This feeding method
                        guarantees that your cat receives a well-rounded diet, packed with essential nutrients
                        needed for a lively and healthy life. Not only does this approach promote dental health by
                        keeping their teeth clean and sharp, but it also mimics their natural eating habits.</span>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        When exploring whole prey options for your cat, the diverse selection caters to their innate
                        preferences and dietary requirements. Fish provides a bounty of omega-3 fatty acids for
                        healthy skin and coat, while chicken or chicks offer lean protein crucial for muscle growth
                        and maintenance. For those seeking to add more variety to their cat's diet, small mammals
                        like hamsters and mice offer both physical and mental stimulation, resembling the diet a cat
                        would have in the wild. Additionally, pigeons present a unique taste and texture experience
                        for your feline friend.</span>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span>
                            By incorporating whole prey into your cat's diet, you not only satisfy their carnivorous
                            cravings but also engage them in a more natural eating behavior, stimulating their
                            senses and encouraging their hunting instincts. This holistic approach to feeding
                            contributes significantly to their overall well-being, ensuring a happy and healthy
                            life.
                        </span>
                    </li>
                </ul>


            </div>
        </div><!-- Features Item -->

        <div class="row gy-4 align-items-stretch justify-content-between features-item" style="row-gap: 30px;">

            <div class="col-lg-5 d-flex order-1-class justify-content-center flex-column " data-aos="fade-up">
                <h3>
                    Dry Food
                </h3>
                <p>
                    When it comes to feeding our feline friends, the choice between dry and wet food can be a
                    puzzling one. Dry food, often seen as a convenient and economical option, has its fair share of
                    supporters and detractors. Understanding the benefits and drawbacks of dry food can help cat
                    owners make informed decisions about their pet's nutrition.
                </p>
                <h3>Pros of Dry Food for Cats:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <h4 class="pt-3 pb-1" style="font-size: 19px;">Convenience</h4>

                    <li>
                        <i class="bi bi-check"></i> <span>
                            One of the most significant advantages of dry cat food is
                            its convenience. It can be left out for cats to graze on throughout the day without the
                            risk of spoilage, making it a favored choice for pet owners with busy schedules.
                        </span>
                    </li>

                    <h4 class="pt-3 pb-1" style="font-size: 19px;">Cost-Effectiveness:</h4>

                    <li>
                        <i class="bi bi-check"></i> <span>
                            Generally, dry food is more economical than wet food. It
                            comes in larger packages that last longer, offering more meals per dollar spent.
                        </span>
                    </li>
                    <h4 class="pt-3 pb-1" style="font-size: 19px;">Dental Health: </h4>

                    <li>
                        <i class="bi bi-check"></i> <span>
                            Some veterinarians argue that the crunchy texture of dry
                            food can help reduce tartar build-up on a cat's teeth, potentially contributing to
                            better overall dental health.
                        </span>
                    </li>
                    <h4 class="pt-3 pb-1" style="font-size: 19px;">Less Messy:</h4>

                    <li>
                        <i class="bi bi-check"></i> <span>
                            Dry food is less messy and easier to clean up than wet
                            food, which can be a significant advantage in maintaining a clean feeding area.
                        </span>
                    </li>
                </ul>


            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-6 order-2-class  align-items-center features-img-bg " data-aos="zoom-out">
                <img src="~/webassets/images/elements/step-37.jpeg" class="img-fluid" alt="">
            </div>
        </div>

    </div>

